# Audit stack configuration for ONE host. Copy to `audit-setup/.env` on that
# host and fill it in. Gitignored -- it carries the shipper's AWS key and the
# store passwords.
#
# scripts/deploy_audit.sh generates this per host from audit-setup/hosts.yaml
# and the credentials audit_bootstrap_aws.sh created, so it is normally not
# written by hand. It is documented here because every value has to be knowable
# by someone setting a host up without that script.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE SHORT VERSION
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Exactly ONE value is required:
#
#     AUDIT_HOST_LABEL=my-box
#
# A .env with that single line installs a complete, working, local-only stack:
# kernel sensor + shipper + queryable hot store, no AWS account, no archive.
# Every other value below has a default, is generated, or is optional. Run
# `./install.sh --preflight` and it will tell you which mode you are in.
#
# Each variable is marked REQUIRED, DEFAULTED (used if you leave it blank),
# GENERATED (install.sh invents one and writes it back here) or OPTIONAL.

# --- identity --------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED.
# This host's name in every event row and every S3 key. Alphanumeric, dash and
# underscore only. STABLE FOR THE LIFE OF THE HOST -- renaming splits its
# history in two and nothing joins the halves back together. Not defaulted from
# the machine's hostname on purpose: a hostname can change, and this must not.
AUDIT_HOST_LABEL=

# DEFAULTED: collector.
# collector = tetragon + vector + clickhouse (the queryable store; one per network)
# sensor    = tetragon + vector (ships rows to the collector, archive straight to S3)
AUDIT_ROLE=collector

# DEFAULTED: /opt/audit/data.
# Where this host keeps the export log, the shipper's buffers and (collector
# only) the store. Must be on a disk with room; see install.sh's preflight,
# which refuses to install a collector with less than 20G free.
AUDIT_DATA_DIR=/opt/audit/data

# --- archive (S3) -- OPTIONAL, and off by default --------------------------
# Leave AUDIT_BUCKET EMPTY and the stack runs LOCAL-ONLY: install.sh renders the
# shipper config with no aws_s3 sink at all, nothing leaves the machine, and
# everything is still captured, redacted and queryable in ClickHouse.
#
# What you give up by leaving it empty is the tamper-evidence property, and it
# is the point of the whole design: with an archive, a host that is fully
# compromised can stop NEW records from being written but cannot alter or delete
# what already landed in S3, and the gap it leaves is what the off-box heartbeat
# alarms on. Without one, root on this host can erase its own history.
#
# Local-only is the right mode for trying the stack out, for a lab box, or for a
# machine whose record does not need to survive the machine. To turn the archive
# on later: run scripts/audit_bootstrap_aws.sh once for the network, put the
# bucket and the shipper key here, and re-run ./install.sh.
#
# The bucket audit_bootstrap_aws.sh creates is versioned, encrypted, and under
# an Object Lock in COMPLIANCE mode.
AUDIT_BUCKET=
# DEFAULTED: us-east-1. Only read when AUDIT_BUCKET is set.
AWS_REGION=us-east-1

# OPTIONAL. The `audit-shipper` credential. It can PutObject under raw/ and
# NOTHING else -- no list, no read, no delete, no retention change. Assume this
# key is readable by anything that compromises this host; that is why it is
# scoped this way, and why a compromised host cannot erase what it already
# shipped. Leave both blank with a bucket set and the shipper falls back to
# whatever ambient AWS credentials the host has, which is usually not what you
# want; install.sh warns about it.
AUDIT_SHIPPER_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
AUDIT_SHIPPER_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# NOT a setting. The shipper's disk buffer is fixed at 1 GiB per sink in
# vector/vector.yaml, for the same reason as the ingest port: Vector parses its
# YAML before interpolating and only interpolates strings, so a typed numeric
# field cannot read an environment variable. Change it in that file.
# It is the amount of store-or-network outage a host can ride out without
# dropping anything; audit data is never dropped quietly.

# --- store -----------------------------------------------------------------
# DEFAULTED on a collector: http://audit-clickhouse:8123 (the store's own
# container on the audit docker network). REQUIRED on a sensor, where it is the
# collector's address, reachable over WireGuard or an SSH tunnel -- never over
# the open LAN. A sensor is never given a default here, because guessing would
# silently point it at a ClickHouse that is not there.
AUDIT_CLICKHOUSE_ENDPOINT=http://audit-clickhouse:8123
# DEFAULTED: 8124. Loopback-only port on the collector, for install.sh and the
# gateway.
AUDIT_CLICKHOUSE_HTTP_PORT=8124

# GENERATED when blank. install.sh invents a random password for any of these
# that is empty and writes it back into this file, so re-running is idempotent
# and the value stays findable. A password that IS set is never touched.
#
# Three users, three jobs (audit-setup/clickhouse/users.d/audit.xml):
#   ingest  INSERT only. Lives on every monitored host. Treat as public.
#   reader  SELECT only, capped rows and time. What astation and the AI get.
#   admin   everything, loopback only. Schema and TTLs. Never leaves the box.
#
# A sensor only ever needs `ingest`; the other two are the collector's.
AUDIT_CLICKHOUSE_INGEST_PASSWORD=
AUDIT_CLICKHOUSE_READER_PASSWORD=
AUDIT_CLICKHOUSE_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# --- phase 2 ---------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT a setting. The shipper's ingest listener is fixed at 8686 in
# vector/vector.yaml, because Vector will not interpolate an environment
# variable into a socket-address field. It is recorded here only so the value
# is findable: the astation plugin posts to http://audit-vector:8686/ingest,
# on the audit docker network, published to nothing.
